values ('1f0aed56-e98a-5e95-bb07-2c8bda75a360', 'wg6-informative-writing-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'write-g6-informative-writing'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Why Bread Dough Rises$q$, $q$(1) Many kinds of bread start as a heavy lump of dough and come out of the oven light and airy. (2) This change happens because of yeast. (3) Yeast is a tiny living thing, a type of fungus, that is too small to see without a microscope.

(4) When a baker mixes yeast with flour and water, the yeast begins to feed on sugars in the flour. (5) As it feeds, it gives off a gas called carbon dioxide. (6) The gas forms bubbles inside the dough. (7) Stretchy strands in the dough, made from a protein called gluten, trap the bubbles like tiny balloons. (8) As more bubbles form, the dough gets bigger.

(9) Temperature affects how fast this happens. (10) Yeast works best in a warm kitchen. (11) In a cold room, the dough may take many hours to rise. (12) Heat that is too strong, however, can kill the yeast. (13) In the oven, the bubbles expand one last time before the yeast dies and the bread sets into its final shape.$q$, 'draft')
