        '6-8', $q$The Kite That Would Not Fly$q$, $q$(1) Every spring, our town holds a kite festival on Harlow Hill, and this year my cousin Mateo and I entered a kite we had built ourselves. (2) We had made it from paper, two thin sticks, and a long tail of ribbon scraps. (3) When we reached the top of the hill, the sky was already full of dragons, fish, and giant diamonds. (4) Mateo held the kite while I backed away with the string. (5) I shouted, and he let go. (6) The kite rose a few feet, spun in a circle, and crashed into the grass. (7) We tried again and again, but it kept diving nose first. (8) Paper is made from wood pulp that is pressed into thin sheets. (9) A woman flying a red box kite nearby walked over to watch us. (10) "Your tail is too short," she said kindly. "It needs more weight to keep the kite steady."

(11) Mateo and I looked at each other. (12) We tied on every extra ribbon from my backpack, plus the string from Mateo's hoodie. (13) This time, when he let go, the kite climbed straight up. (14) It wobbled once, then settled high above the hill beside the dragons and fish.

(15) We went home at four o'clock.$q$, 'draft')
