values ('6ce50a73-3902-5105-a73f-b63381d80b71', 'wg6-paragraph-transitions-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'write-g6-paragraph-transitions'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Telling Time Before Clocks$q$, $q$(1) Long before anyone wore a watch, people still needed to know the time. (2) Early farmers watched the sun rise and set to plan their work in the fields. (3) Later, people invented the sundial, a flat plate with a pointer that casts a shadow. (4) As the sun moved across the sky, the shadow moved around the plate and pointed to the hour. (5) ____, a sundial was useless on cloudy days and at night.

(6) To solve this problem, some people built water clocks. (7) A water clock was a container with a tiny hole near the bottom. (8) Water dripped out slowly and steadily, so the level inside dropped little by little. (9) Marks on the inside of the container showed how many hours had passed. (10) Many kitchens today have a small clock on the oven. (11) Each of these tools helped people measure their days a little more exactly.$q$, 'draft')
