-- Passage wg7-informative-writing-p06
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('ab66dfe8-e09b-5d56-8a33-c7c4514a5a06', 'wg7-informative-writing-p06', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'write-g7-informative-writing'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Stories Told in Shadow$q$, $q$(1) Long before movies, people in many parts of the world told stories with shadows. (2) Shadow puppetry is a form of theater in which flat figures are held between a light and a screen. (3) The audience sits on the other side of the screen and sees only the dark shapes the puppets cast. (4) Traditions of shadow theater have existed for centuries in places such as China, Indonesia, Turkey, and India.

(5) Making a shadow puppet takes careful planning. (6) First, puppet makers cut figures from thin materials such as leather, cardboard, or heavy paper. (7) They often punch small holes in the figure to let light through, which creates details like the pattern on a robe. (8) Next, they connect the arms and legs with small pins so the parts can move. (9) Finally, they attach thin rods that the puppeteer holds from below or behind.

(10) Performing is a skill of its own. (11) A puppeteer must keep each figure flat against the screen, because a figure that tilts away becomes fuzzy. (12) Moving a puppet closer to the light makes its shadow grow larger. (13) Puppeteers use this trick to make a character seem to come toward the audience. (14) My little brother likes to make bunny shapes with his hands on his bedroom wall.

(15) Today, schools and theaters still use shadow puppets to share stories.$q$, 'draft')
