-- Passage wg7-informative-writing-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('2e94298e-72ca-5056-8a0b-71a1f64155d7', 'wg7-informative-writing-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'write-g7-informative-writing'), null,
        '6-8', $q$How Barcodes Work$q$, $q$(1) Almost every package in a grocery store has a small pattern of black and white stripes printed on it. (2) This pattern is a barcode. (3) A barcode is a way of writing a number so that a machine can read it quickly. (4) Different widths of black bars and white spaces stand for different digits.

(5) At the checkout counter, a scanner shines a beam of light across the barcode. (6) The black bars absorb most of the light, while the white spaces reflect it back. (7) A sensor in the scanner measures this pattern of reflected light and turns it into a number. (8) The store's computer then looks up the number in a database, which is an organized collection of information. (9) The name and price of the item appear on the screen.

(10) Barcodes help stores in several ways. (11) Cashiers can check out shoppers much faster than when they typed each price by hand. (12) Mistakes also become less common. (13) In addition, the computer keeps track of every item sold, so managers know when to order more. (14) The grocery store near my house is open until ten at night.$q$, 'draft')
