-- Passage wg7-informative-writing-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('7dd37f1b-72a9-57a2-8e0d-63ea868ea5ab', 'wg7-informative-writing-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'write-g7-informative-writing'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Why Bread Dough Rises$q$, $q$(1) A lump of bread dough can double in size while it sits on a kitchen counter. (2) This change happens because of yeast. (3) Yeast is a tiny living fungus, so small that thousands could fit on the head of a pin. (4) When a baker mixes yeast into flour and water, the yeast begins to feed on sugars in the flour.

(5) As the yeast feeds, it gives off a gas called carbon dioxide. (6) The dough also contains gluten, a stretchy material that forms when flour and water are mixed and kneaded. (7) Gluten acts like a net. (8) It traps the bubbles of gas, so the dough puffs up like a balloon. (9) If the dough is not kneaded enough, the gluten net is weak, and much of the gas escapes.

(10) Temperature affects how fast the dough rises. (11) Yeast works best in a warm place. (12) Cold slows it down, so dough in a refrigerator may take a whole night to rise. (13) Very hot water, however, can kill the yeast before it does its job. (14) For this reason, many recipes tell bakers to use water that feels warm but not hot.

(15) Bread is good.$q$, 'draft')
