        '6-8', $q$The Short Life of the Pony Express$q$, $q$(1) In 1860, sending a letter across the western United States could take weeks or even months. (2) A new mail service called the Pony Express promised to cut that time to about ten days. (3) Riders carried mail in leather pouches from Missouri to California, a distance of nearly two thousand miles. (4) Along the route, workers kept stations where riders could switch to rested horses every ten to fifteen miles. (5) Each rider galloped about seventy-five miles before handing the mail to a fresh rider. (6) This system kept the mail moving day and night.

(7) The Pony Express became famous, but it did not last long. (8) In October 1861, workers finished a telegraph line that connected the East and West coasts. (9) ____, a message that once took ten days by horse could now cross the country in minutes. (10) The Pony Express closed just two days later. (11) Today, people remember it as a bold experiment in speed and teamwork.$q$, 'draft')
